<h1>Counterpoint: making two melodies work together</h1>

<p class="lede">
  Counterpoint is the craft of writing two or more melodies that sound good
  at the same time <em>and</em> each sound like their own melody. It's not
  about following old rules for their own sake. It's about independence.
  When two lines stop being independent, your ear hears one thick line
  instead of two, and the music loses a voice.
</p>

<h2>The one rule that matters most</h2>
<p>
  Most counterpoint guidelines boil down to one idea:
  <strong>don't let two voices accidentally fuse.</strong>
  The fastest way to fuse them is
  <span class="term" title="Two voices a 5th apart moving the same direction, the classic counterpoint no-no.">parallel fifths</span>
  and
  <span class="term" title="Two voices an octave apart moving together, collapsing two lines into one.">parallel octaves</span>:
  when two voices move in the same direction while staying a fifth or an
  octave apart, those intervals are so acoustically "empty" that the voices
  lock together. The opposite,
  <span class="term" title="Two voices moving in opposite directions. The most independent, strongest motion.">contrary motion</span>,
  is what keeps lines feeling separate and alive.
  (Why fifths and octaves in particular? That's arithmetic.
  <a href="/math/">See the math</a>.)
</p>

